1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,300
Russia's nuclear satellite.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:04,520
Launch window: sixty days.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:09,181
German reporters say Western intelligence believes Russia

4
00:00:09,201 --> 00:00:10,821
has built a satellite designed

5
00:00:10,841 --> 00:00:12,243
to carry a nuclear weapon.

6
00:00:12,483 --> 00:00:15,803
It is reportedly parked near Moscow, ready to launch.

7
00:00:16,043 --> 00:00:17,827
Intelligence shared inside NATO

8
00:00:17,847 --> 00:00:19,863
this month says: within sixty days.

9
00:00:20,103 --> 00:00:21,593
Moscow says none of it is true.

10
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:22,473
So who is right?

11
00:00:22,713 --> 00:00:24,426
And what would one nuclear blast

12
00:00:24,446 --> 00:00:25,833
in orbit do to your phone,

13
00:00:25,853 --> 00:00:27,403
your maps, and your internet?

14
00:00:27,643 --> 00:00:28,927
In the next few minutes:

15
00:00:28,947 --> 00:00:30,447
what the reports really say,

16
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:31,913
and why Russia denies them.

17
00:00:32,153 --> 00:00:33,782
Then, the experiment from nineteen

18
00:00:33,802 --> 00:00:35,673
sixty-two that shows what could happen.

19
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:38,308
Stay to the end, because the scariest number

20
00:00:38,328 --> 00:00:40,503
comes from a study published this month.

21
00:00:41,017 --> 00:00:42,237
First, the reports.

22
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:44,077
The story comes from a joint

23
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:46,277
investigation by three German outlets.

24
00:00:46,517 --> 00:00:49,527
They cite Western intelligence and German military sources.

25
00:00:49,767 --> 00:00:50,567
None of them are named.

26
00:00:50,807 --> 00:00:53,557
The satellite is reportedly called Sputnik S.

27
00:00:53,797 --> 00:00:54,909
It was built years ago,

28
00:00:54,929 --> 00:00:56,337
and it is stored near Moscow.

29
00:00:56,577 --> 00:00:58,965
Intelligence passed between NATO countries this

30
00:00:58,985 --> 00:01:01,477
month says a launch could come within sixty days.

31
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:03,891
Germany's military Space Command reportedly

32
00:01:03,911 --> 00:01:05,677
sees a launch as early as December.

33
00:01:05,917 --> 00:01:06,387
But hold on.

34
00:01:06,627 --> 00:01:08,389
It is not even clear which country

35
00:01:08,409 --> 00:01:09,857
collected that intelligence.

36
00:01:10,097 --> 00:01:11,847
And no launch date has been confirmed.

37
00:01:12,087 --> 00:01:14,017
No nuclear payload has been shown.

38
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:15,380
Now, Moscow's answer.

39
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:17,114
The Kremlin spokesman said he

40
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:18,680
had not even seen the reports.

41
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,780
And Russia has played this scene before.

42
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:23,502
In February twenty twenty-four, Washington warned

43
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:25,850
about a Russian nuclear anti-satellite weapon.

44
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:27,833
President Putin replied that Russia

45
00:01:27,853 --> 00:01:30,050
is against putting nuclear weapons in space.

46
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:31,985
The Kremlin called the warning a trick

47
00:01:32,005 --> 00:01:33,610
to make Congress approve more money.

48
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:35,607
Two months later, Russia used its veto

49
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:37,430
at the United Nations Security Council.

50
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:40,581
The resolution only asked countries to respect the nineteen

51
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:42,121
sixty-seven Outer Space Treaty,

52
00:01:42,141 --> 00:01:43,910
which bans nuclear weapons in orbit.

53
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:45,460
Dozens of countries backed it.

54
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:46,870
Russia vetoed it anyway.

55
00:01:47,110 --> 00:01:48,850
Moscow called the text political.

56
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:51,169
Its own draft, banning all weapons in space,

57
00:01:51,189 --> 00:01:51,980
was rejected too.

58
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,380
But the denial is not the strangest part.

59
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:57,300
The strangest part happened sixty-four years ago.

60
00:01:57,817 --> 00:01:59,807
July ninth, nineteen sixty-two.

61
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:02,586
The United States fires a nuclear warhead

62
00:02:02,606 --> 00:02:04,647
into space from a Pacific island.

63
00:02:04,887 --> 00:02:06,857
It is called Starfish Prime.

64
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:08,547
One point four megatons,

65
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:11,427
about one hundred times the power of Hiroshima.

66
00:02:11,667 --> 00:02:14,517
It explodes about four hundred kilometers above the Pacific.

67
00:02:14,757 --> 00:02:16,447
Right where satellites orbit today.

68
00:02:16,687 --> 00:02:18,517
Nobody expected what came next.

69
00:02:18,757 --> 00:02:21,677
The blast trapped radiation in Earth's magnetic field.

70
00:02:21,917 --> 00:02:24,027
It created an artificial radiation belt.

71
00:02:24,267 --> 00:02:25,956
That belt knocked out Telstar,

72
00:02:25,976 --> 00:02:28,747
the first satellite to broadcast live television.

73
00:02:28,987 --> 00:02:30,807
It also took down Britain's first satellite.

74
00:02:31,047 --> 00:02:32,597
At least six satellites failed.

75
00:02:32,837 --> 00:02:34,417
The belt lingered for years.

76
00:02:34,657 --> 00:02:35,906
Back then, there were about

77
00:02:35,926 --> 00:02:37,317
two dozen satellites in orbit.

78
00:02:37,557 --> 00:02:39,521
Today, around thirteen thousand

79
00:02:39,541 --> 00:02:41,377
circle us in low orbit alone.

80
00:02:41,883 --> 00:02:43,163
Which brings us to the study.

81
00:02:43,403 --> 00:02:46,395
This month, researchers from the University of Wisconsin,

82
00:02:46,415 --> 00:02:48,983
Madison and Princeton posted a simulation online.

83
00:02:49,223 --> 00:02:51,279
They imagined a one-megaton blast,

84
00:02:51,299 --> 00:02:54,453
five hundred kilometers up, over the South Atlantic.

85
00:02:54,693 --> 00:02:56,213
Smaller than Starfish Prime.

86
00:02:56,453 --> 00:02:58,366
On day one, a quarter of the satellites

87
00:02:58,386 --> 00:02:59,803
in low orbit are knocked out.

88
00:03:00,043 --> 00:03:02,243
After three days: seventy-eight percent.

89
00:03:02,483 --> 00:03:04,233
After a week: ninety-eight percent.

90
00:03:04,473 --> 00:03:07,073
By day fifteen: more than ninety-nine percent.

91
00:03:07,313 --> 00:03:08,053
Starlink.

92
00:03:08,293 --> 00:03:09,373
Weather satellites.

93
00:03:09,613 --> 00:03:10,623
Military craft.

94
00:03:10,863 --> 00:03:12,913
And those satellites carry more than television.

95
00:03:13,153 --> 00:03:18,183
They carry navigation, payments, weather forecasts, and military communications.

96
00:03:18,423 --> 00:03:19,433
Now the caveats.

97
00:03:19,673 --> 00:03:21,743
That study is not yet peer reviewed.

98
00:03:21,983 --> 00:03:24,207
The results depend on where the blast happens,

99
00:03:24,227 --> 00:03:25,913
and how hardened each satellite is.

100
00:03:26,153 --> 00:03:27,712
A weapon detonated much higher

101
00:03:27,732 --> 00:03:29,553
would mostly hit satellites nearby.

102
00:03:29,793 --> 00:03:32,203
And Russian and Chinese satellites would suffer too.

103
00:03:32,443 --> 00:03:35,064
Even in twenty twenty-four, reporters asked why Russia

104
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:36,384
would need a nuclear weapon

105
00:03:36,404 --> 00:03:37,753
just to destroy a satellite.

106
00:03:38,283 --> 00:03:40,023
NATO is not taking the risk lightly.

107
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:41,772
In April twenty twenty-five,

108
00:03:41,792 --> 00:03:43,191
NATO chief Mark Rutte said

109
00:03:43,211 --> 00:03:44,773
the alliance knew of reports.

110
00:03:45,013 --> 00:03:48,113
Russia, he said, was considering nuclear weapons in space.

111
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:49,853
He called that very worrying.

112
00:03:50,093 --> 00:03:52,513
Russia's space technology is outdated, he said.

113
00:03:52,753 --> 00:03:53,895
A nuclear weapon could

114
00:03:53,915 --> 00:03:55,003
be a way to catch up.

115
00:03:55,243 --> 00:03:56,353
So there are two readings.

116
00:03:56,593 --> 00:03:58,433
One: the intelligence is right,

117
00:03:58,453 --> 00:04:00,053
and a launch is weeks away.

118
00:04:00,293 --> 00:04:02,864
Two: it is another leak and another pressure play,

119
00:04:02,884 --> 00:04:05,713
like twenty twenty-four, when no weapon was ever shown.

120
00:04:05,953 --> 00:04:07,923
Either way, December is the date to watch.

121
00:04:08,163 --> 00:04:09,633
So here is my question for you.

122
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,351
Do you think Russia would really

123
00:04:12,371 --> 00:04:13,730
put a nuclear weapon in orbit?

124
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:15,002
Tell me in the comments,

125
00:04:15,023 --> 00:04:16,540
and watch the next video on your screen.
